---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Cta from '../../components/Cta.astro';
import DownloadButton from '../../components/DownloadButton.astro';
import { appSchema } from '../../lib';

const rows = [
  ['How it works', 'HTTP proxy: traffic is routed through Charles', 'Per-app network control, no proxy'],
  ['Throttling', 'Throttles everything going through the proxy', 'Throttles only the apps you choose'],
  ['HTTPS', 'Install and trust Charles’s root certificate to see HTTPS', 'Sees simulator requests with no certificate or proxy settings'],
  ['Failures', 'Rewrite, map and breakpoint requests', 'Airplane Mode, refused connections, time-outs, dropped connections, outages that come and go, fail or mock one endpoint'],
  ['Offline in the simulator', 'Not a device-like offline state', 'NWPathMonitor and SCNetworkReachability report no network'],
  ['Physical devices', 'Yes, by pointing the device at the proxy', 'Mac apps and iOS Simulator apps'],
];
---
<Base
  title="Charles Proxy throttling alternative for iOS Simulator and Mac"
  description="Lagsmith vs. Charles Proxy for network throttling: throttle one app instead of everything through a proxy, without installing certificates, with Airplane Mode and failure modes."
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Compare', href: '/network-link-conditioner-alternative' }, { name: 'Lagsmith vs. Charles Proxy', href: '/vs/charles-proxy' }]}
  jsonLd={[appSchema()]}
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap narrow">
      <h1>Lagsmith vs. Charles Proxy for throttling</h1>
      <p class="lead">Charles Proxy is a long-standing HTTP debugging proxy, and its throttling is popular with iOS developers. If throttling is what you mostly use Charles for, Lagsmith is a simpler Charles Proxy throttling alternative: per app, no proxy and no certificates.</p>
      <div class="actions"><DownloadButton /></div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="table-compare">
          <thead><tr><th></th><th>Charles Proxy</th><th>Lagsmith</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{rows.map(([k, a, b]) => <tr><td>{k}</td><td>{a}</td><td>{b}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="wrap narrow">
      <h2>When Charles is the better fit</h2>
      <p>Charles is great for deep HTTP debugging: breakpoints, rewriting, mapping requests to local files, and inspecting traffic from physical devices. Many developers use both.</p>
      <h2>When to use Lagsmith</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>You want to slow down one app, not everything going through a proxy.</li>
        <li>You don’t want to install and trust a root certificate in each simulator.</li>
        <li>You need real offline behaviour, time-outs and dropped connections, not only slow responses.</li>
        <li>You want an AI agent or UI test to control the network.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Lagsmith exports requests as HAR, which opens in Charles. See also <a href="/network-link-conditioner-alternative">Lagsmith vs. Network Link Conditioner</a> and <a href="/vs/proxyman">Lagsmith vs. Proxyman</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <Cta />
</Base>
